NAME
    cp - copy files and directory trees

SYNOPSIS
    cp SOURCE DEST
    cp [-r|-R] SOURCE DEST
    cp [-r|-R] SOURCE... DIRECTORY

DESCRIPTION
    Copies regular files with bounded streamed I/O. With -r or -R, traverses
    directory trees iteratively. Symbolic links encountered during traversal
    are reproduced without following them. Traversal metadata grows fallibly
    to documented runtime ceilings; file payload size is not staged in memory.

    The final operand is the destination. It is classified once, before any
    copy begins. With more than one source the destination must already be an
    existing directory; otherwise the command fails having copied nothing,
    which is what stops several sources from overwriting each other in one
    file. With exactly one source, an existing directory receives the source
    under its own base name and any other destination names the copy itself.

    A source whose base name is empty, . or .. is rejected. Sources sharing a
    base name resolve last writer wins. Every source is attempted; the exit
    status reflects the first failure.

EXAMPLES
    cp notes.txt /vol/shared/notes.txt
    cp *.txt /vol/shared
    cp -r /vol/shared/build /vol/shared/build-backup
